Chapter: SmpTss

 Every core has its own task register, checked from inside the guest.

 A double fault is delivered on the stack named by IST1 in the task
 state segment the task register points at. The task register is per-core
 and a task state segment cannot be shared, so each core carries its own:
 the descriptor for core i sits at selector 24 + i * 16, and each core
 loads that selector and points its IST1 at a 2 KB emergency stack of its
 own.

 Application processors used to load no task register at all. A fault on
 one had no stack to arrive on and disappeared as a triple fault -- no
 dump, no output, nothing. That cost nothing while no application
 processor had ever executed a guest instruction, which was true until the
 multi-core bring-up was fixed. It costs a great deal now.

 Each core reads its task register back with str after loading it and
 stores what the processor actually accepted at cell 79872 + core * 8. A
 descriptor the processor takes and one it rejects look the same from the
 outside, and a core whose task register reads zero has no emergency stack
 and would still vanish. So this reads back what the hardware did, not
 what the boot code intended.

Section: Probe

  tr-of-core : Integer -> Integer
  tr-of-core (i) = atomic-load (79872 + i * 8)

  expected-tr : Integer -> Integer
  expected-tr (i) = 24 + i * 16

  all-cores-loaded : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  all-cores-loaded (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if tr-of-core i == expected-tr i then all-cores-loaded (i + 1) n
    else False

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let cores = atomic-load 4088
    in let n = if cores < 1 then 1 else cores
    in act
      print-line-uni ("cores: " & show n)
      print-line-uni ("bsp tr: " & show (tr-of-core 0))
      print-line-uni ("ap1 tr: " & show (tr-of-core 1))
      print-line-uni (if all-cores-loaded 0 n
                      then "every core loaded its own task register"
                      else "A CORE HAS NO TASK REGISTER")
    end
  end
